exports[`rendered output 00-start-here.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-start" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc" data-source-range="2:3"><h1>Dimm City Design Guide</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The Dimm City design system for Gutterpress: how to write a Dimm City book in markdown, and what every macro, component, and page template looks like when it prints. Every example in this guide is live — the markdown shown is rendered by the same package the Field Guide uses, so what you see is what a book gets.</p>
<div class="dg-toc">
<h2>Part 1 — Using the system</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#ch-writing">Writing a Page</a> — markers, attributes, the markdown the system styles</li>
<li><a href="#ch-typography">Typography</a> — the three faces, the type scale, heading chrome</li>
<li><a href="#ch-palette">Color</a> — paper, fire and HUD tokens, and how to retheme</li>
<li><a href="#ch-layout">Layout</a> — columns, panels, sidebars, floats, break control</li>
<li><a href="#ch-text">Text &amp; Callouts</a> — ledes, flavor, pull quotes, the alert family, tape, tags</li>
<li><a href="#ch-panels">Panels, Cards &amp; Data</a> — blocks, sidebar boxes, definitions, procedures, outcomes, cards, gear, tables, stat blocks</li>
<li><a href="#ch-specialty">The Specialty System</a> — specialties, learning paths, skill cards</li>
<li><a href="#ch-templates">Page Templates</a> — chapter openers, contents, credits, catalog and profile pages</li>
<li><a href="#ch-publishing">Publishing</a> — preview, build, validate</li>
<li><a href="#ch-reference">Reference</a> — every marker on one page; options, classes, tokens</li>
<li><a href="#ch-variants">Variant Gallery</a> — every element with more than one variant, side by side: all ten skill trees, skill cards, intros, cards</li>
</ol>
<h2>Part 2 — A book, page by page</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#ch-examples">About the examples</a></li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-front-matter">Front Matter</a> — contents, credits, introduction</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-chapter-opener">Chapter Opener</a> — chapter-start page, fiction excerpt, citizen file walkthrough</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-specialty-overview">Specialty Overview</a> — the specialty card grid</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-specialty-profile">Specialty Profile</a> — intro, art, a learning path with skill cards</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-rules">Rules &amp; Mechanics</a> — sections, definitions, conditions, the outcome ladder</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-dm-npcs">Dream Master Pages</a> — DM notes, NPC stat blocks</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-gear-tech">Gear &amp; Tech</a> — gear entries, callouts, an ego-point ladder</li>
</ol>
</div>
<hr data-source-range="40:41">
<h2>How to read this guide</h2>
<p>Every component page follows the same shape. The markdown you type is shown in a box labelled <strong>You write</strong>; directly beneath it, labelled <strong>Result</strong>, is that exact markdown rendered by the package. Options, variants, and the tokens you can retune follow in a short table. When a component only makes sense on a whole page, the page is shown in Part 2 and linked from the component.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="46:53" class="language-markdown">@lede

This is what you type.

@end-lede
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-intro" data-source-range="58:59"><p>This is what you type.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Three rules carry through the whole system, and they are worth knowing before anything else:</p>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="68:69"><strong>A marker is a line that starts with <code>@</code>.</strong> <code>@lede</code> opens a block, <code>@end-lede</code> closes it. Leave a blank line on each side of every marker.</li>
<li data-source-range="69:70"><strong>Markers take attributes</strong> — <code>#id</code>, <code>.class</code>, and <code>key=&quot;value&quot;</code> — in any order after the name: <code>@section panel #rules .dc-snug</code>.</li>
<li data-source-range="70:72"><strong>Classes on a parent change everything inside it.</strong> <code>@specialty .augmerc</code> gives every card, path, and intro within it the Augmerc shape and color; nothing is set card by card.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Pick a section</h2>
<p>Start from what you are writing, not from a class name: find it on the left and write what is on the right. The same table is in the package’s README.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="76:77">
<tr data-source-range="76:77">
<th>I am writing…</th>
<th>Use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="78:97">
<tr data-source-range="78:79">
<td>Rules or reference text under a heading</td>
<td><code>@section panel</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="79:80">
<td>The same, kept on one page</td>
<td><code>@section panel .dc-avoid-split</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="80:81">
<td>Unboxed text that carries the accent rail</td>
<td><code>@section flat</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="81:82">
<td>A short note set off from the text</td>
<td><code>@section aside</code> (or <code>@sidebar-box</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="82:83">
<td>A term and its definition</td>
<td><code>@definition</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="83:84">
<td>A boxed note, warning, DM note, vibe, origin or visit</td>
<td><code>@callout …</code> (<code>@dm-note</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="84:85">
<td>A box in one of four registers: panel, slate, shard, codex</td>
<td><code>@block …</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="85:86">
<td>A box floated beside the text</td>
<td><code>@sidebar</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="86:87">
<td>Fiction, lore, a chapter-opener story</td>
<td><code>@fiction-excerpt</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="87:88">
<td>A creature or NPC</td>
<td><code>@npc-stat</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="88:89">
<td>Gear, a vehicle, a drug, an augment</td>
<td><code>@gear</code> (and <code>@callout gear label=&quot;SPECS&quot;</code> for the specs)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="89:90">
<td>A roll-result ladder</td>
<td><code>@outcome</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="90:91">
<td>Flaw, ideal or dream choices</td>
<td><code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code>, <code>@dreams</code> (each a run of <code>@card</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="91:92">
<td>The Citizen File field list</td>
<td><code>@citizen-walkthrough</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="92:93">
<td>The specialty catalog</td>
<td><code>@card-grid</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="93:94">
<td>A specialty, its learning paths and skill cards</td>
<td><code>@specialty</code>, <code>@learning-path</code>, <code>@skill</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="94:95">
<td>Grouping, with no styling</td>
<td><code>@section</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="95:96">
<td>A section that overflows by a line or two</td>
<td>add <code>.dc-snug</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="96:97">
<td>Where it sits</td>
<td><code>@page chapter-start</code>, <code>intro</code>, <code>credits</code>, <code>contents</code>, <code>citizen-file</code> (and core’s <code>blank</code>, <code>full-bleed</code>); <code>@chapter rules</code>, <code>lore</code>, <code>citizen-file</code>, <code>specialty</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The named components are also words on <code>@section</code> (<code>@npc-stat</code> is <code>@section npc</code>, <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> is <code>@section fiction</code>); either spelling renders the same. Sections split across pages; add <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> to keep one whole. A page or chapter word never draws a box of its own: it re-tunes the colour and spacing of the sections inside it. <a href="#ch-writing" data-gp-source-token="[Writing a Page](#ch-writing)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Writing a Page</a> covers the grammar, <a href="#ch-panels" data-gp-source-token="[Panels &amp; Cards](#ch-panels)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Panels &amp; Cards</a> the sections, and <a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a> the page and chapter words.</p>
<h2>Quick start</h2>
<p>Install the package into a book, write, and preview:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="104:109" class="language-sh">gutterpress new &quot;My Book&quot; --preset dtrpg
gutterpress ext add gp-dimm-city my-book
gutterpress preview my-book
</code></pre>
<p>Or, in the Gutterpress app: open the book, then Project settings → Features → <em>Install from npm</em> → <code>gp-dimm-city</code>. The package’s macros appear in the snippet picker.</p>
<p>Working on the package itself? This guide loads it from the repository it lives in (<code>extensions: - ../</code> in <code>manifest.yaml</code>), so <code>npm run dev</code> at the repository root previews every change to <code>plugin.js</code> or a stylesheet here, live. The developer loop is in the repository’s <code>docs/developing.md</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 01-writing-a-page.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-writing" data-ch="1" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-1" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 1</p>
<h1>Writing a Page</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">A Dimm City book is plain markdown with a small vocabulary of <code>@</code> markers. Markdown carries the text; markers draw the structure — chapters, pages, sections, and the components the rest of this guide catalogs. This chapter is the grammar.</p>
<h2>The structure markers</h2>
<p>Gutterpress itself provides the structural markers. They wrap everything that follows until the matching <code>@end-</code> marker, the next marker of the same kind, or the end of the file.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="14:15">
<tr data-source-range="14:15">
<th>Marker</th>
<th>Closes with</th>
<th>What it is</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="16:23">
<tr data-source-range="16:17">
<td><code>@chapter #id ch=&quot;N&quot;</code></td>
<td>next <code>@chapter</code> / end of file</td>
<td>A chapter. <code>ch=&quot;N&quot;</code> prints the <code>c.N</code> footer on every page of it</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<td><code>@page .class</code></td>
<td>next <code>@page</code></td>
<td>A page. Classes pick a page template (<a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="18:19">
<td><code>@spread</code></td>
<td>next <code>@spread</code> / <code>@page</code></td>
<td>Two facing pages kept together</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td><code>@section [word] [.class]</code></td>
<td><code>@end-section</code></td>
<td>A region on the page. Bare, it is a plain box; <code>@section panel</code> is the card</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td><code>@columns 2</code></td>
<td><code>@end-columns</code></td>
<td>Set what is between in 2 to 5 columns</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="21:22">
<td><code>@page-break</code></td>
<td>—</td>
<td>Force a new page here</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="22:23">
<td><code>@column-break</code></td>
<td>—</td>
<td>Force the next column, inside <code>@columns</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A chapter is one file. A <code>@chapter</code> line opens each file, and the chapter ends where the file ends.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="26:37" class="language-markdown">@chapter #ch-augmerc ch=&quot;3&quot;

@section panel

## The Augmerc

Muscle for hire. The difference is gear, grafts, and how much of them is still original.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="42:43"><h2>The Augmerc</h2>
<p>Muscle for hire. The difference is gear, grafts, and how much of them is still original.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>@section panel</code> is the card: cream substrate, accent rail, clipped corner, shadow, and the first <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> as a title bar. A bare <code>@section</code> is core’s plain box: it groups what is inside it and adds no look, so the text sits on the page. Between them are <code>@section flat</code> (the rail, no card) and <code>@section aside</code> (an inset note); <a href="#ch-panels" data-gp-source-token="[Panels &amp; Cards](#ch-panels)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Panels &amp; Cards</a> shows all three. For columns, wrap the content in <code>@columns 2</code>.</p>
<p>Sections split across pages by default. Add <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> to keep one whole.</p>
<p>The named components have a marker of their own, and each is also a word on <code>@section</code>: <code>@npc-stat</code> is <code>@section npc</code>, <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> is <code>@section fiction</code>, <code>@citizen-walkthrough</code> is <code>@section walkthrough</code>, <code>@card-grid</code> is <code>@section cards</code>, and <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> and <code>@dreams</code> are <code>@section flaws</code>, <code>@section ideals</code> and <code>@section dreams</code>. Either spelling renders the same. Not sure which to use? <a href="#ch-start" data-gp-source-token="[Pick a section](#ch-start)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Pick a section</a> maps what you are writing to the marker.</p>
<h2>Attributes</h2>
<p>Every marker takes the same attribute grammar after its name, in any order: <code>#id</code> sets an id, <code>.class</code> adds a class, <code>key=value</code> or <code>key=&quot;quoted value&quot;</code> sets a named option.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="62:67" class="language-markdown">@section panel #core-loop .dc-avoid-split

@callout warning label=&quot;Heat is shared&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Standard markdown elements take attributes too, with braces at the end of the element: <code>## Title {.dc-spray}</code>, <code>![alt](img.png){.dc-img-float-right}</code>, and <code>@skill {.dc-avoid-split}</code> for a class on a component macro.</p>
<h2>The blank-line rule</h2>
<p>Put a blank line before and after every marker. Gutterpress reads a marker even when it sits straight under a list item or a line of text, but the blank line keeps the page readable and keeps a marker from being mistaken for prose.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="74:81" class="language-markdown">@callout note

Pick a Spec. Spend 6 Spec Points.

@end-callout
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note" data-callout="note" data-source-range="86:87"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p>Pick a Spec. Spend 6 Spec Points.</p>
</div></div>
<h2>Markdown the system styles</h2>
<p>Nothing below needs a class or a marker. The package styles the standard elements the way a Dimm City page expects.</p>
<h3>Headings</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="100:106" class="language-markdown"># Chapter title
## Section heading
### Sub-section
#### Item heading
</code></pre>
<p><code>#</code> opens a chapter or a specialty. <code>##</code> is a section with an accent rule. <code>###</code> labels a column or a sub-topic. <code>####</code> is an item: a skill, a gear entry, a stat-block name. In a two-column layout, <code>###</code> and <code>####</code> are the workhorses — <code>#</code> and <code>##</code> are too wide for a 3.5-inch column.</p>
<h3>Emphasis and inline code</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="111:116" class="language-markdown">**Bold** lands in burnt orange.
*Italic* shifts to warm smoke.
\`SysChk\` marks a keyword that is also a mechanic.
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p><strong>Bold</strong> lands in burnt orange. <em>Italic</em> shifts to warm smoke. <code>SysChk</code> marks a keyword that is also a mechanic.</p>
</div>
<h3>Lists</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="125:132" class="language-markdown">- A loose list
- of short items

1. An ordered list
2. for sequences
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<ul>
<li data-source-range="135:136">A loose list</li>
<li data-source-range="136:138">of short items</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="138:139">An ordered list</li>
<li data-source-range="139:141">for sequences</li>
</ol>
</div>
<h3>Tables</h3>
<p>Pipe tables get the header band, alternating fills, and the data type size. Roll tables, option tables, and stat references are all plain tables.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="147:153" class="language-markdown">| Band | Range |
|---|---|
| **Reach** | Adjacent |
| **Near** | Same room |
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<table>
<thead data-source-range="156:157">
<tr data-source-range="156:157">
<th>Band</th>
<th>Range</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="158:160">
<tr data-source-range="158:159">
<td><strong>Reach</strong></td>
<td>Adjacent</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="159:160">
<td><strong>Near</strong></td>
<td>Same room</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Blockquote</h3>
<p>A plain <code>&gt;</code> quote is an epigraph: accent rail, italic body. Inside a skill card the first <code>&gt;</code> line is the card’s flavor.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="167:172" class="language-markdown">&gt; Every city has a language.
&gt;
&gt; — Hollis Vance
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<blockquote>
<p data-source-range="175:176">Every city has a language.</p>
<p data-source-range="177:178">— Hollis Vance</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<h3>Definition list</h3>
<p>A term line and a <code>: definition</code> line render as the on-brand glossary form.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="185:192" class="language-markdown">Tick
: A heartbeat. Used in ability text to mean *immediately*.

Reach
: Close enough to touch.
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<dl>
<dt data-source-range="195:195">Tick</dt>
<dd data-source-range="196:198">A heartbeat. Used in ability text to mean <em>immediately</em>.</dd>
<dt data-source-range="198:198">Reach</dt>
<dd data-source-range="198:201">Close enough to touch.</dd>
</dl>
</div>
<h3>Smart typography and footnotes</h3>
<p><code>--</code> becomes an en dash, <code>---</code> an em dash, <code>...</code> an ellipsis, and straight quotes curl. Footnotes use <code>[^label]</code> inline and <code>[^label]: text</code> anywhere in the file; they render at the end of the content flow.</p>
<h3>Images</h3>
<p>An image on its own line is a figure. Place it in the flow where it should sit; floats and framing are classes, described in <a href="#ch-layout" data-gp-source-token="[Layout](#ch-layout)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Layout</a>.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="211:214" class="language-markdown">![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png)
</code></pre>
<h2>A complete page</h2>
<p>Putting the pieces together — the shape of an ordinary body page in a Dimm City book:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="219:242" class="language-markdown">@chapter #ch-rules ch=&quot;4&quot;

@section panel

## Augment Points

@columns 2

**AP** is your push. If an ability lists an AP cost, you spend it and the effect happens.

@column-break

- **0 AP** — Free to use. Not always safe.
- **1–X AP** — You choose how hard you push.

@end-columns

@end-section

&gt; [!NOTE]
&gt; You start every dream with 10 AP.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="247:248"><h2>Augment Points</h2>
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="251:252"><p><strong>AP</strong> is your push. If an ability lists an AP cost, you spend it and the effect happens.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="255:256"></div>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="257:258"><strong>0 AP</strong> — Free to use. Not always safe.</li>
<li data-source-range="258:260"><strong>1–X AP</strong> — You choose how hard you push.</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div class="dc-alert dc-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p data-source-range="264:266">You start every dream with 10 AP.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 02-typography.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter typography dg-guide" id="ch-typography" data-ch="2" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-2" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 2</p>
<h1>Typography</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">Three faces, each with one job. lixdu is the display face for chapter and section headings and banners; Tomorrow is the mono face for tabs, chips, counters, and code; Titillium Web carries every line of body, flavor, and quote copy.</p>
<h2>The type scale</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="12:13">
<tr data-source-range="12:13">
<th>Element</th>
<th>You write</th>
<th>Size · Face</th>
<th>Role</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="14:24">
<tr data-source-range="14:15">
<td>H1</td>
<td><code># Title</code></td>
<td>26pt bold · lixdu</td>
<td>Chapter and specialty openers</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="15:16">
<td>H2</td>
<td><code>## Heading</code></td>
<td>20pt bold · lixdu</td>
<td>Section breaks, with an accent rule</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="16:17">
<td>H3</td>
<td><code>### Sub-heading</code></td>
<td>16pt · lixdu</td>
<td>Column labels and sub-topics</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<td>H4</td>
<td><code>#### Item</code></td>
<td>13pt uppercase · lixdu</td>
<td>Skill names, gear names, stat-block names</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="18:19">
<td>Body</td>
<td>a paragraph</td>
<td>12pt · Titillium Web</td>
<td>All prose</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td>Flavor</td>
<td><code>&gt; [!FLAVOR]</code></td>
<td>12pt italic · Titillium Web</td>
<td>In-world voice</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td>Chevron banner</td>
<td><code># Title {.dc-chevron}</code></td>
<td>H1 scale, angled fill</td>
<td>One per chapter opener</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="21:22">
<td>Spray banner</td>
<td><code>## Title {.dc-spray}</code></td>
<td>H2 scale, spray-paint fill</td>
<td>Learning paths, major topic breaks</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="22:23">
<td>Card tab</td>
<td>emitted by <code>@skill</code></td>
<td>9pt · Tomorrow</td>
<td>Skill-card tab labels</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="23:24">
<td>Tag</td>
<td><code>&lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;</code></td>
<td>8pt · Tomorrow</td>
<td>Keyword and cost chips</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>The three faces</h2>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>lixdu</strong> — display · <code>--font-display</code> · headings set uppercase, H1 26pt, leading 1.35, tracking 0.2em</p>
<p class="dg-face-display">ABCDEFGHIJKLM<br>NOPQRSTUVWXYZ<br>0123456789</p>
<p class="dg-face-display dg-face-set">Wired to kill</p>
</div>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>Titillium Web</strong> — body · <code>--font-body</code> · 12pt, leading 1.5 (18pt), tracking 0.005em</p>
<p class="dg-face-body">ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz 0123456789 &amp;?!</p>
<p class="dg-face-body dg-face-set">Corporate enforcers earn their grafts in blood and overtime; street muscle runs cheaper and lasts longer than anyone admits. <em>Italic carries the in-world voice,</em> and <strong>bold marks a rule.</strong></p>
</div>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>Tomorrow</strong> — labels · <code>--font-mono</code>, <code>--font-tab</code> · tabs, chips and counters at 8–9pt, uppercase, tracking 0.1em</p>
<p class="dg-face-tab">ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz 0123456789</p>
<p class="dg-face-tab dg-face-set">AUG1.3 · 2 AP · SYSCHK 9–13</p>
</div>
<h2>Heading hierarchy</h2>
<p>Each level rendered at its print size:</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<h1>Chapter Title</h1>
<h2>Section Heading</h2>
<h3>Sub-section Label</h3>
<h4>Item Heading</h4>
</div>
<h2>Level and look</h2>
<p>A heading’s level is its place in the outline, and the outline never skips a level: <code>#</code> is followed by <code>##</code>, never <code>###</code>. Gutterpress flags a skip when it builds. When the page needs a smaller label than the outline allows, keep the level and set the look with <code>.dc-h3</code>, <code>.dc-h4</code>, <code>.dc-h5</code> or <code>.dc-h6</code>:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="65:74" class="language-markdown"># Cybernetics, Weapons, and Gear

## Useful Items {.dc-h3}

### Base Reality {.dc-h4}

#### Dreamer {.dc-h5}
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<h2 class="dc-h3">Useful Items</h2>
<h3 class="dc-h4">Base Reality</h3>
<h4 class="dc-h5">Dreamer</h4>
</div>
<p>The look follows the class everywhere: an <code>##</code> with <code>.dc-h3</code> that opens a section takes the subordinate section bar, and a <code>###</code> with <code>.dc-h4</code> inside a section takes no accent rule. On a paragraph, the class sets a line in heading type without adding it to the outline.</p>
<h2>Heading chrome</h2>
<p>Three classes dress a heading. The chevron opens a chapter, the spray breaks a major topic, and the spec-tweak rule flags an optional mechanic; <code>.dc-no-top</code> pulls it tight against whatever is above it.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="93:100" class="language-markdown"># Augmerc {.dc-chevron}

## Biting Distance {.dc-spray}

### Spec Tweak: Wired to Kill {.dc-spec-tweak .dc-no-top}
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="105:106"><h1 class="dc-chevron">Augmerc</h1>
<h2 class="dc-spray">Biting Distance</h2>
<h3 class="dc-spec-tweak dc-no-top">Spec Tweak: Wired to Kill</h3>
</div></div>
<h2>Body and flavor</h2>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>Twelve-point Titillium Web carries all running narrative. Corporate enforcers earn their grafts in blood and overtime; street muscle runs cheaper and lasts longer than anyone admits. Notice the leading, the x-height, and how weight shifts when a word is <strong>bolded</strong> or <em>italicized</em> mid-sentence.</p>
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="123:125">See an opening, ya take it. Best time to hit 'em is when they think it's over.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Flavor keeps the body size and switches to italic and <code>--ink-smoke</code>. Never set flavor in bold italic — it reads as urgency, not voice.</p>
<p class="dg-dont-label">Don’t</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-dont">
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="134:136"><em><strong>See an opening, ya take it. Best time to hit 'em is when they think it's over.</strong></em></p>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Column-safe headings:</strong> in a two-column layout use <code>###</code> and <code>####</code>. <code>#</code> and <code>##</code> at full print size exceed a 3.5-inch column.</p>
<h2>Smart typography</h2>
<p>The renderer converts ASCII shortcuts as you type: <code>--</code> is an en dash --, <code>---</code> an em dash —, <code>...</code> an ellipsis …, and “straight quotes” curl. No special syntax, no Unicode entry.</p>
<h2>Font tokens</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="147:148">
<tr data-source-range="147:148">
<th>Token</th>
<th>Face</th>
<th>Used for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="149:154">
<tr data-source-range="149:150">
<td><code>--font-display</code></td>
<td>lixdu</td>
<td>H1–H3, banners</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="150:151">
<td><code>--font-body</code></td>
<td>Titillium Web</td>
<td>Body prose, flavor, quotes</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="151:152">
<td><code>--font-mono</code></td>
<td>Tomorrow</td>
<td>Code, counters, tabular data</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="152:153">
<td><code>--font-tab</code></td>
<td>Tomorrow</td>
<td>Short label text: card tabs, stat labels</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="153:154">
<td><code>--font-quote</code></td>
<td>Titillium Web</td>
<td>Italic quotes and attributions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The fonts ship in the package and embed in the PDF at build time; a book never installs them.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 03-palette.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter palette dg-guide" id="ch-palette" data-ch="3" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-3" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 3</p>
<h1>Color</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">Cream paper, dark ink, and two accent registers. Creaturepunk <strong>fire</strong> — crimson, orange, rust, amber — for printed lore, ability text, and danger. <strong>HUD digital</strong> — blue and magenta — for cybernetics, tech, and system chrome. The page is never black, and fire and HUD never share one element.</p>
<h2>Paper &amp; ink</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>BG</strong><code>#c8c5bf</code><code>--bg</code><code>21/17/21/5</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Cream</strong><code>#f0eee9</code><code>--paper-cream</code><code>6/5/7/0</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Light</strong><code>#e2ded7</code><code>--paper-light</code><code>12/10/14/0</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Aged</strong><code>#c8c2b8</code><code>--paper-aged</code><code>20/18/25/6</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink on-dark"><strong>Ink</strong><code>#1a1512</code><code>--ink</code><code>42/49/45/99</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink-smoke on-dark"><strong>Ink Smoke</strong><code>#4d4339</code><code>--ink-smoke</code><code>32/47/55/100</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink-dust on-dark"><strong>Ink Dust</strong><code>#665b4e</code><code>--ink-dust</code><code>27/44/62/99</code></div>
</div>
<p>The page background (<code>--bg</code>) is the wall; a brick texture is painted over it by the package’s <code>@page</code> rule. Paper surfaces are what sits on the wall — cards, callouts, panels. Components are posters and displays hung on the wall: hard edges, decisive separation, never a soft tint that lets the texture through.</p>
<h2>Creaturepunk fire</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-crimson on-dark"><strong>Crimson</strong><code>#e1261c</code><code>--crimson</code><code>0/95/100/17</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-blood on-dark"><strong>Blood</strong><code>#901a12</code><code>--blood</code><code>0/92/100/45</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-orange on-dark"><strong>Orange</strong><code>#d4500a</code><code>--orange</code><code>0/75/100/24</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-rust on-dark"><strong>Rust</strong><code>#b23a12</code><code>--rust</code><code>0/83/100/40</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-amber on-light"><strong>Amber</strong><code>#7a5a20</code><code>--amber</code><code>1/29/100/80</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-deep-rust on-dark"><strong>Deep Rust</strong><code>#6a1a08</code><code>--deep-rust</code><code>0/84/91/63</code></div>
</div>
<h2>HUD digital</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue on-dark"><strong>HUD Blue</strong><code>#1f6f94</code><code>--hud-blue</code><code>100/28/0/46</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue-bright on-light"><strong>HUD Bright</strong><code>#00bcd4</code><code>--hud-blue-bright</code><code>100/0/13/0</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue-dim border on-light"><strong>HUD Dim</strong><code>#7ab8d0</code><code>--hud-blue-dim</code><code>60/11/9/5</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-magenta on-dark"><strong>HUD Magenta</strong><code>#c026d3</code><code>--hud-magenta</code><code>38/100/0/5</code></div>
</div>
<h2>Surfaces &amp; borders</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-panel border on-light"><strong>HUD Panel</strong><code>#eeece8</code><code>--hud-panel</code><code>7/5/7/0</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-surface-tint-3 border on-light"><strong>Surface 3</strong><code>#f2f0ec</code><code>--surface-tint-3</code><code>5/4/6/0</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-surface-orange-tint border on-light"><strong>Orange Tint</strong><code>#d8cfb4</code><code>--surface-orange-tint</code><code>15/14/32/3</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-border-hairline on-light"><strong>Hairline</strong><code>#c8b898</code><code>--border-hairline</code><code>17/20/45/11</code></div>
</div>
<p><code>--paper-stain</code> (<code>#b0a89c</code>) is a textured fill; <code>--border-hairline</code> is a rule line. Other rules use the ink scale directly.</p>
<h2>Usage rules</h2>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="58:59">Crimson is the dominant accent — one crimson element per composition. Stack further emphasis with orange.</li>
<li data-source-range="59:60">HUD blue and magenta mean cybernetics or tech. Never mix fire and HUD on the same element.</li>
<li data-source-range="60:61">Paper is for raised elements. The wall is the canvas; cream is the surface.</li>
<li data-source-range="61:63">Keep CMYK coverage under 280% on coated stock and 240% on uncoated. The fire palette runs hot — proof on paper before a print run.</li>
</ul>
<p>Running text goes on a paper surface, never straight on the wall: the brick texture under body type cuts its contrast and breaks up the letterforms.</p>
<p class="dg-dont-label">Don’t</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-dont">
<p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence. Nothing here is safe, and nothing here is free.</p>
</div>
<p class="dg-result">Do</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="77:78"><p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence. Nothing here is safe, and nothing here is free.</p>
</div></div>
<h2>Retheming a book</h2>
<p>Every component exposes its colors and shapes as <code>--dc-*</code> custom properties, with defaults in the package’s <code>styles/dc-component-defaults.css</code>. A book never edits the package: it lists one sheet of its own under <code>styles:</code> and resets tokens there, either for the whole book at <code>:root</code> or for one chapter at the chapter’s id. The book’s sheet is unlayered and the package sits in its own cascade layer, so the override wins at any specificity.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="90:101" class="language-css">/* my-book/styles/overrides.css */
:root {
  --dc-flavor-accent: var(--hud-blue);        /* every flavor rail, whole book */
}

#ch-gear {
  --dc-section-accent: var(--amber);          /* section chrome in one chapter */
  --dc-alert-border: var(--rust);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The brand tokens themselves — <code>--crimson</code>, <code>--hud-blue</code>, <code>--paper-cream</code>, the fonts — live in <code>styles/dc-palette.css</code> and reset the same way. The full token-to-component map is in <a href="#ch-reference" data-gp-source-token="[Reference](#ch-reference)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Reference</a>.</p>
<p>Do not write bare <code>.dc-*</code> rules in a book to change a component; that fights the package on every update. Reset the component’s tokens on a context selector instead.</p>
<h2>CMYK</h2>
<p>Each swatch’s third line is its CMYK build (C/M/Y/K, percent), converted from the hex through CGATS21_CRPC1 — the coated press profile gutterpress embeds in a PDF/X build — with relative colorimetric intent. Treat them as starting points: RGB to CMYK is not one-to-one for saturated colors, so request a physical proof before the full print run.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 04-layout.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter layout dg-guide" id="ch-layout" data-ch="4" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-4" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 4</p>
<h1>Layout</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">How content is arranged inside a page: columns, sidebars, floated art, and the handful of classes that control where things break. Everything here is a marker, a class on <code>@section</code>, <code>@page</code>, or an image.</p>
<h2>Columns</h2>
<p><code>@columns</code> is Gutterpress’s one way to set content in columns: <code>@columns 2</code> to <code>@columns 5</code>, closed by <code>@end-columns</code>. Text fills the first column and overflows into the next; <code>@column-break</code> forces the next column.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="15:26" class="language-markdown">@columns 2

Left column. Text fills top to bottom and overflows right.

@column-break

Right column.

@end-columns
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="31:32"><p>Left column. Text fills top to bottom and overflows right.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="35:36"></div>
<p>Right column.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>@columns 3</code> is the same marker with a 3. A run’s columns balance, so a short run ends level. Whatever wants columns goes inside <code>@columns</code>: paragraphs, a list, a run of entries. The components in this guide take it the same way, for example <code>@columns 2</code> inside a <code>@skill</code>, a <code>@card-grid</code> or an <code>@npc-stat</code>.</p>
<h2>A card with columns</h2>
<p>A column run inside a <code>@section panel</code> sits in the standard card: the leading <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> is the heading bar at full width, and <code>@columns 2</code> below it splits the body.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="49:66" class="language-markdown">@section panel

## The City

@columns 2

Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence.

@column-break

Nothing here is safe. Nothing here is free.

@end-columns

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="71:72"><h2>The City</h2>
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="75:76"><p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="79:80"></div>
<p>Nothing here is safe. Nothing here is free.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h2>Sidebar</h2>
<p><code>@sidebar</code> floats a reference panel to the right at 38% width; body text wraps on its left. Give it running prose on both sides so it never strands in white space.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="93:106" class="language-markdown">When the Dreamers push past Too Far, the table starts asking how movement works.

@sidebar

### Distance, Fast

Three bands, no grid. **In Reach** is one swing away. **Nearby** is a burned Move. **Too Far** might as well be the other side of the ether.

@end-sidebar

Keep the bands fictional, not metric. The Dream Master sets the band; the dice decide whether you close it.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>When the Dreamers push past Too Far, the table starts asking how movement works.</p>
<div class="dc-sidebar" data-source-range="113:114"><h3>Distance, Fast</h3>
<p>Three bands, no grid. <strong>In Reach</strong> is one swing away. <strong>Nearby</strong> is a burned Move. <strong>Too Far</strong> might as well be the other side of the ether.</p>
</div><p>Keep the bands fictional, not metric. The Dream Master sets the band; the dice decide whether you close it. A Dreamer who wants to cross two bands in one turn is telling you they’re willing to spend everything to get there — let them, and let it cost.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="page dg-doc chapter-4" data-source-range="125:126"><h2>Floated images</h2>
<p>An image on its own line is a figure. <code>.dc-img-float-right</code> or <code>.dc-img-float-left</code> floats it at 44% of the column with text wrapping around it; leave a blank line after the paragraph that should clear it.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="131:136" class="language-markdown">![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png){.dc-img-float-right}

Blasts lit the dusk like glitchfire. Bolts and teeth and claws tangled mid-air.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p class="dc-img-wrapper"><img src="img/scavenger.png" alt="Scavenger" class="dc-img-float-right" data-gp-source-token="![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0"></p>
<p>Blasts lit the dusk like glitchfire. Bolts and teeth and claws tangled mid-air. Neon signs cracked. Alleyways bled smoke. Debris rained in bursts. This wasn’t about glory — it was turf. It was pride. It was blood memory, raw and ugly, of family torn away by their rival.</p>
<p>DimmCitz scattered, vanished into bolted dens and reinforced rooftops. The air stank of scorched fur, ozone, and cordite.</p>
</div>
<p>A <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> places its first image for you — see <a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a>.</p>
<h2>Break control</h2>
<p>Callouts keep themselves whole by default: they move to the next page rather than split. Sections and skill cards do the opposite: they split where the page ends, at a sensible place (a skill card never opens with only its tab, and an ability row is never cut). Two classes change that, and a third forces a break.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="155:156">
<tr data-source-range="155:156">
<th>You write</th>
<th>Effect</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="157:162">
<tr data-source-range="157:158">
<td><code>@section panel .dc-avoid-split</code></td>
<td>Keep this section whole: it moves to the next page rather than splitting</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="158:159">
<td><code>@section .gp-no-break</code></td>
<td>Core’s own never-split class, for a section of any kind</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="159:160">
<td><code>@skill {.dc-avoid-split}</code></td>
<td>Keep every card of this skill whole: it moves to the next page rather than splitting</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="160:161">
<td><code>## Heading {.gp-break-before}</code></td>
<td>Start a new page before this element</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="161:162">
<td><code>@page-break</code></td>
<td>Start a new page here</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Reach for <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> on a short section that would read badly in two halves; a section kept whole leaves a dead zone at the foot of the page before it. On a skill card the same class costs more: a card is a third to half a page, so keeping one whole leaves a page mostly empty. Prefer splitting content at a logical boundary first — <code>@continue</code> for a long skill card.</p>
<h2>Tighter spacing</h2>
<p><code>.dc-snug</code> halves the space between paragraphs and list items inside a section or page. Reach for it when a section overflows by a line or two and the last line would otherwise strand on a page of its own. Type size and leading are untouched.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="169:178" class="language-markdown">@section panel .dc-snug
### Ideal

Paragraph and list-item gaps shrink to 0.04in here.
@end-section

@page .dc-snug
</code></pre>
<h2>Edge to edge</h2>
<p><code>.dc-flush</code> removes a component’s side margins so it runs the full column. It is read by tape, stat blocks, sticker chains, and <code>@outcome flush</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 05-text-and-callouts.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-text" data-ch="5" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-5" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 5</p>
<h1>Text &amp; Callouts</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The components that live inside running prose: the lede that opens a chapter, flavor and pull quotes for in-world voice, the alert family for notes a reader must not miss, and the tape, tags, and roll chip that punctuate a page.</p>
<h2>Lede</h2>
<p>The opening paragraph of a chapter or major section, set larger on its own panel.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="15:22" class="language-markdown">@lede

The city didn't go quiet — it got loud. You came to Dimm City to disappear, and instead the whole block learned your name.

@end-lede
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-intro" data-source-range="27:28"><p>The city didn’t go quiet — it got loud. You came to Dimm City to disappear, and instead the whole block learned your name.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-intro-bg</code>, <code>--dc-intro-accent</code>.</p>
<h2>Flavor</h2>
<p>In-world voice: italic, accent-railed, spoken from inside the fiction. Inside a <code>@skill</code> card the first <code>&gt;</code> line is flavor automatically; anywhere else, tag the quote.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="41:45" class="language-markdown">&gt; [!FLAVOR]
&gt; Down here we don't ask what you were before the grafts. We ask what's left.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="50:52">Down here we don't ask what you were before the grafts. We ask what's left.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-flavor-accent</code>, <code>--dc-flavor-accent-width</code>, <code>--dc-flavor-color</code>.</p>
<h2>Pull quote</h2>
<p>A large-format excerpt with rules above and below. One per chapter, to let a single line breathe. The attribution goes after a blank <code>&gt;</code> line.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="61:67" class="language-markdown">&gt; [!PULLQUOTE]
&gt; The rig braces and answers every swing.
&gt;
&gt; Field manual, second draft
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-pullquote dc-flush">
<p data-source-range="72:74">The rig braces and answers every swing.</p>
<p data-source-range="75:76">Field manual, second draft</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-pullquote-accent</code>, <code>--dc-pullquote-accent-soft</code>, <code>--dc-pullquote-bg</code>.</p>
<h2>Alerts</h2>
<p>One shell, seven registers. Write a GFM alert — a blockquote whose first line is <code>[!TYPE]</code> — and the label, color, and rail come with the type. Use the blockquote form for a single paragraph; for several paragraphs, a list, or a custom label, use the <code>@callout</code> block below.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="86:110">&gt; [!NOTE]
&gt; A character may hold no more than one Signature Augment at a time.

&gt; [!WARNING]
&gt; Trauma Patches stabilize a dying character but do not restore HP.

&gt; [!DM]
&gt; If a player hasn't chosen starting gear by the end of session zero, hand them a Scavenger Pack and move on.

&gt; [!VIBE]
&gt; The rain in Dimm City never quite stops — it just changes color under the signage.

&gt; [!ORIGIN]
&gt; You didn't choose the street — the street chose you.

&gt; [!VISIT]
&gt; The Neon Bazaar doesn't close. If you can name it, someone here is selling a knockoff of it two stalls down.

&gt; [!GEAR]
&gt; **Ripper Blades (Mk II)**
&gt;
&gt; Melee. Damage 1d8+STR. *Serrated:* on a critical hit, the target bleeds for 1d4.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-tall">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p data-source-range="115:117">A character may hold no more than one Signature Augment at a time.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-note warning"><span class="dc-alert-label">Warning</span>
<p data-source-range="118:120">Trauma Patches stabilize a dying character but do not restore HP.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-dm-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Dream Master Note</span>
<p data-source-range="121:123">If a player hasn't chosen starting gear by the end of session zero, hand them a Scavenger Pack and move on.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-vibe-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Vibe</span>
<p data-source-range="124:126">The rain in Dimm City never quite stops — it just changes color under the signage.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-origin-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Origin</span>
<p data-source-range="127:129">You didn't choose the street — the street chose you.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-visit-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Visit</span>
<p data-source-range="130:132">The Neon Bazaar doesn't close. If you can name it, someone here is selling a knockoff of it two stalls down.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-gear-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Gear</span>
<p data-source-range="133:135"><strong>Ripper Blades (Mk II)</strong></p>
<p data-source-range="136:137">Melee. Damage 1d8+STR. <em>Serrated:</em> on a critical hit, the target bleeds for 1d4.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3>Which alert</h3>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="142:143">
<tr data-source-range="142:143">
<th>Type</th>
<th>Who it is for</th>
<th>Register</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="144:151">
<tr data-source-range="144:145">
<td><code>NOTE</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A rules clarification</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="145:146">
<td><code>WARNING</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A rule with consequences, in amber</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="146:147">
<td><code>DM</code></td>
<td>The Dream Master only</td>
<td>Guidance, hooks, adjudication</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="147:148">
<td><code>VIBE</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>Atmosphere and narrative voice, full width</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="148:149">
<td><code>ORIGIN</code></td>
<td>Players, in second person</td>
<td>Backstory addressed to the character</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="149:150">
<td><code>VISIT</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A location, present tense, before an encounter</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="150:151">
<td><code>GEAR</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A named item: weapon, armor, notable equipment</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Tokens shared by the family: <code>--dc-alert-bg</code>, <code>--dc-alert-border</code>, <code>--dc-alert-fg</code>, <code>--dc-alert-label-color</code>, <code>--dc-alert-border-width</code>.</p>
<h2>Callout block</h2>
<p>The multi-paragraph form of an alert. A variant word after <code>@callout</code> picks the register; <code>label</code> replaces the default label.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="159:168" class="language-markdown">@callout warning label=&quot;Heat is shared&quot;

**Heat is shared, not personal.** When any member of the crew draws Corporate attention, the whole crew's Heat track ticks up.

At Heat 3, expect a response. At Heat 5, the crew is actively hunted, and every public roll carries a complication.

@end-callout
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note warning" data-callout="warning" data-label="Heat is shared" data-source-range="173:174"><span class="dc-alert-label">Heat is shared</span>
<p><strong>Heat is shared, not personal.</strong> When any member of the crew draws Corporate attention, the whole crew’s Heat track ticks up.</p>
<p>At Heat 3, expect a response. At Heat 5, the crew is actively hunted, and every public roll carries a complication.</p>
</div></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="183:184">
<tr data-source-range="183:184">
<th>Option</th>
<th>Values</th>
<th>Default</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="185:187">
<tr data-source-range="185:186">
<td>a variant word (<code>@callout vibe</code>)</td>
<td><code>note</code> <code>warning</code> <code>dm</code> <code>vibe</code> <code>origin</code> <code>visit</code> <code>gear</code></td>
<td><code>note</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="186:187">
<td><code>label</code></td>
<td>any text</td>
<td>the variant’s label</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Dream Master note</h2>
<p><code>@dm-note</code> is shorthand for <code>@callout dm</code>, for the longer GM asides — scene setups, adjudication, design notes.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="192:201" class="language-markdown">@dm-note label=&quot;Running the Setup&quot;

The fixer offering this job doesn't know it's bait — they're a cut-out, paid to pass the contract along and ask no questions.

If the players investigate before accepting, they can smell the trap with a Streetwise roll, DC 14.

@end-dm-note
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-dm-note" data-callout="dm" data-label="Running the Setup" data-source-range="206:207"><span class="dc-alert-label">Running the Setup</span>
<p>The fixer offering this job doesn’t know it’s bait — they’re a cut-out, paid to pass the contract along and ask no questions.</p>
<p>If the players investigate before accepting, they can smell the trap with a Streetwise roll, DC 14.</p>
</div></div>
<h2>Tape</h2>
<p>A torn-tape strip for a section break or a label between groups — tiers of NPCs, parts of a list.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="221:224" class="language-markdown">&lt;div class=&quot;dc-tape&quot;&gt;Operators&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-tape">Operators</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-tape-bg</code>, <code>--dc-tape-border</code>, <code>--dc-tape-color</code>. Add <code>dc-flush</code> (<code>&lt;div class=&quot;dc-tape dc-flush&quot;&gt;Label&lt;/div&gt;</code>) to run it edge to edge.</p>
<h2>Dashed rule</h2>
<p>A plain <code>---</code> between entries is a red dashed rule — the separator for gear lists and NPC entries.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="239:246" class="language-markdown">Trauma Kit × 2

---

Signal Jammer (single-use)
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>Trauma Kit × 2</p>
<hr data-source-range="253:254">
<p>Signal Jammer (single-use)</p>
</div>
<h2>Tags and class tags</h2>
<p>Inline pills. <code>.dc-tag</code> is a keyword or cost label; <code>.dc-classtag.&lt;specialty&gt;</code> is a specialty identity with that specialty’s dot. These are raw HTML spans — the one place the system asks for it inline.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="263:268" class="language-markdown">&lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Melee&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Reach 2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Loud&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class=&quot;dc-classtag augmerc&quot;&gt;Augmerc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-classtag wirephreak&quot;&gt;Wirephreak&lt;/span&gt;
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p><span class="dc-tag">Melee</span> <span class="dc-tag">Reach 2</span> <span class="dc-tag">Loud</span></p>
<p><span class="dc-classtag augmerc">Augmerc</span>
<span class="dc-classtag proxy">Proxy</span>
<span class="dc-classtag streetwarden">Streetwarden</span>
<span class="dc-classtag gutterdruid">Gutterdruid</span>
<span class="dc-classtag cybersurgeon">Cybersurgeon</span>
<span class="dc-classtag wirephreak">Wirephreak</span>
<span class="dc-classtag technosorcerer">Technosorcerer</span>
<span class="dc-classtag etherlock">Etherlock</span>
<span class="dc-classtag dualist">Dualist</span>
<span class="dc-classtag generalist">Generalist</span></p>
</div>
<h2>Roll the die</h2>
<p>The text <code>ROLL THE DIE!</code> in prose becomes the die chip automatically, and so do <code>ROLL A DIE!</code>, <code>ROLLS A DIE!</code> and <code>ROLLING THE DIE!</code>: uppercase, with the exclamation mark, as written. Inside a code span, raw HTML or a heading it is left alone. The chip is set inside the line and never splits across two, so it never makes a line taller.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="292:295" class="language-markdown">When an ability says ROLL THE DIE!, roll a d20 and read the outcome ladder. A trap may say ROLL A DIE! or ROLLING THE DIE! instead.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>When an ability says <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span>, roll a d20 and read the outcome ladder. A trap may say <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL A DIE!</span> or <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLLING THE DIE!</span> instead.</p>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-roll-the-die-color</code>, <code>--dc-roll-the-die-border</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 06-panels-and-cards.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-panels" data-ch="6" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-6" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 6</p>
<h1>Panels, Cards &amp; Data</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The enclosures: the section variants, four block registers, the sidebar box, definition and glossary forms, the numbered procedure, the outcome ladder, choice cards, gear entries, tables, and the stat blocks a Dream Master runs from.</p>
<h2>Section</h2>
<p>A <code>@section</code> groups what is inside it. Bare, it is core’s plain box and adds no look; a word after it picks the look. Three are general and work anywhere. Sections split across pages by default; <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> keeps one whole. One accent knob, <code>--dc-section-accent</code>, colours the rail and the title bar of all three. For the named components (<code>@npc-stat</code>, <code>@gear</code>, <code>@outcome</code> and the rest), <a href="#ch-start" data-gp-source-token="[Pick a section](#ch-start)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Pick a section</a> maps what you are writing to the marker.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="15:44">@section panel

## Rules Panel

The card: a cream substrate, an accent rail, a clipped corner, a shadow, and the first \`##\` or \`###\` as a title bar.

@end-section

@section flat

The rail on a plain host, for text that should read as a continuation of the page.

@end-section

@section aside

### Worth Knowing

A short note set off from the text: a hairline frame, an accent rail, an italic heading.

@end-section

@section

A bare section: no look at all. The text sits on the page.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-tall">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="49:50"><h2>Rules Panel</h2>
<p>The card: a cream substrate, an accent rail, a clipped corner, a shadow, and the first <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> as a title bar.</p>
</div><div class="section dc-section-flat" data-section="flat" data-source-range="57:58"><p>The rail on a plain host, for text that should read as a continuation of the page.</p>
</div><div class="section dc-section-aside" data-section="aside" data-source-range="63:64"><h3>Worth Knowing</h3>
<p>A short note set off from the text: a hairline frame, an accent rail, an italic heading.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<div class="section" data-source-range="75:76"><p>A bare section: no look at all. The text sits on the page.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>panel</code> is the look for rules and reference text with a heading; <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> on it keeps a short panel on one page. <code>aside</code> is the sidebar-box treatment on a section; reach for <code>@sidebar-box</code> itself when the note needs its own divider. Tokens: <code>--dc-section-bg</code>, <code>--dc-section-accent</code>, <code>--dc-section-pad-top</code>, <code>-right</code>, <code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code>, and for <code>panel</code> also <code>--dc-section-surface</code> and <code>--dc-section-shadow</code> (<code>none</code> removes the layer).</p>
<p>The components below that are sections have a marker of their own and a word on <code>@section</code>: <code>@npc-stat</code> (<code>npc</code>), <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> (<code>fiction</code>), <code>@citizen-walkthrough</code> (<code>walkthrough</code>), <code>@card-grid</code> (<code>cards</code>), <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> and <code>@dreams</code>.</p>
<h2>Fiction excerpt</h2>
<p>Fiction, lore, a chapter-opening story. Aged paper instead of a card: no rail, no title bar, narrative leading, and the first image floats left. On a chapter-start page it takes a heavier edge, two ink rules and an offset shadow.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="91:100">@fiction-excerpt

&quot;It's hard being me, but I guess it's the same for anyting sentient in the monoverse, ay?!&quot;

I wuz tearin down an alley, lungs burnin, heart jackhammering like it wanted out.

@end-fiction-excerpt
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-fiction-excerpt" data-source-range="105:106"><p>“It’s hard being me, but I guess it’s the same for anyting sentient in the monoverse, ay?!”</p>
<p>I wuz tearin down an alley, lungs burnin, heart jackhammering like it wanted out.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-fiction-bg</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-line-height</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-rule</code> (top and bottom edge weight), <code>--dc-fiction-shadow</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-art-float</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-art-width</code>.</p>
<h2>Block</h2>
<p>One macro, four registers. The class on the opener picks the silhouette and surface; <code>label</code> fills the title band and can be omitted.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="121:131" class="language-markdown">@block .dc-panel label=&quot;Action Economy&quot;

On your turn you get **one Move and one Action**. Spend Augment Points to push past it.

- **Move:** shift one Distance band. Free.
- **Action:** attack, hack, or trigger an ability.

@end-block
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-block dc-panel" data-label="Action Economy" data-source-range="136:137"><div class="dc-block-title">Action Economy</div>
<p>On your turn you get <strong>one Move and one Action</strong>. Spend Augment Points to push past it.</p>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="140:141"><strong>Move:</strong> shift one Distance band. Free.</li>
<li data-source-range="141:143"><strong>Action:</strong> attack, hack, or trigger an ability.</li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="147:148">
<tr data-source-range="147:148">
<th>Variant</th>
<th>Register</th>
<th>Use for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="149:153">
<tr data-source-range="149:150">
<td><code>.dc-panel</code></td>
<td>HUD blue, hex corners</td>
<td>Player-facing rules summaries, structured data</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="150:151">
<td><code>.dc-slate</code></td>
<td>Dark surface, magenta band</td>
<td>Binding rulings, Dream Master directives</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="151:152">
<td><code>.dc-shard</code></td>
<td>Aged paper, rust band, zine cut</td>
<td>Fiction, atmosphere, setting asides</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="152:153">
<td><code>.dc-codex</code></td>
<td>Pale cyan, octagon corners</td>
<td>Glossaries, lookups, compendium entries</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-block dc-slate" data-label="The Core Loop" data-source-range="156:157"><div class="dc-block-title">The Core Loop</div>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="158:159">The <strong>Dream Master</strong> presents a situation.</li>
<li data-source-range="159:160">The <strong>Dreamers</strong> declare intent.</li>
<li data-source-range="160:162">The <strong>fiction resolves</strong> — sometimes on a die, sometimes on a word.</li>
</ol>
</div><div class="dc-block dc-shard" data-label="The Neon Bazaar" data-source-range="164:165"><div class="dc-block-title">The Neon Bazaar</div>
<p>Hologram haze cuts the gloom you’re stumbling through. Flickering ads claw the air above blades of neon, and the scent of burnt oil rides over hot garbage.</p>
</div><div class="dc-block dc-codex" data-label="Outcome Ladder (d20)" data-source-range="170:171"><div class="dc-block-title">Outcome Ladder (d20)</div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="172:173">
<tr data-source-range="172:173">
<th>Roll</th>
<th>Result</th>
<th>What happens</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="174:179">
<tr data-source-range="174:175">
<td><strong>20</strong></td>
<td>Triumph</td>
<td>Best case, with extra impact</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="175:176">
<td><strong>11–19</strong></td>
<td>Success</td>
<td>You do the thing</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="176:177">
<td><strong>6–10</strong></td>
<td>Hard Choice</td>
<td>You succeed, but it costs you</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="177:178">
<td><strong>2–5</strong></td>
<td>Failure</td>
<td>You don’t get what you wanted</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="178:179">
<td><strong>1</strong></td>
<td>Catastrophe</td>
<td>It goes bad — and then worse</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-block-bg</code>, <code>--dc-block-fg</code>, <code>--dc-block-accent</code>, <code>--dc-block-title-bg</code>, <code>--dc-block-title-fg</code>.</p>
<h2>Sidebar box</h2>
<p>A standalone reference box with its own heading and an internal dashed divider — rules etiquette, a standalone ruling, anything that needs its own boundary.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="191:202" class="language-markdown">@sidebar-box

#### Dice Etiquette

---

Roll your dice in the open. If a die lands off the table, it doesn't count: reroll it.

@end-sidebar-box
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-prose-panel dc-sidebar-box" data-source-range="207:208"><h4>Dice Etiquette</h4>
<hr data-source-range="211:212">
<p>Roll your dice in the open. If a die lands off the table, it doesn’t count: reroll it.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-sidebar-box-surface</code>, <code>--dc-sidebar-box-border</code>, <code>--dc-sidebar-box-accent</code>, <code>--dc-sidebar-box-rail</code>.</p>
<h2>Definition</h2>
<p>A short italic callout for one term — in-world vocabulary, an NPC type, an item category. Stack several in an <code>@columns 2</code> run for a glossary of callouts.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="225:232" class="language-markdown">@definition

**Tick / Tic:** A heartbeat. The space between deciding and doing. Used in ability text to mean *immediate*.

@end-definition
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="237:238"><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="239:240"><p><strong>Tick / Tic:</strong> A heartbeat. The space between deciding and doing. Used in ability text to mean <em>immediate</em>.</p>
</div><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="245:246"><p><strong>Shortly / Shorty:</strong> Roughly a scene. Long enough to cross a district, tend a wound, or shake a tail.</p>
</div><div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="251:252"></div>
<div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="253:254"><p><strong>Fixer:</strong> The person who slides into the booth with a dirty envelope and a job you’ll regret.</p>
</div><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="259:260"><p><strong>Sporo:</strong> A native of Dimm City wearing an animal form.</p>
</div></div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-definition-block-surface</code>, <code>--dc-definition-block-accent</code>, <code>--dc-definition-block-accent-width</code>. For a plain term list, a markdown definition list (<code>Term</code> / <code>: meaning</code>) needs no macro — see <a href="#ch-writing" data-gp-source-token="[Writing a Page](#ch-writing)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Writing a Page</a>.</p>
<h2>Glossary</h2>
<p>A run of <strong>term</strong> — gloss paragraphs as one connected register, the form of an appendix glossary.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="275:284" class="language-markdown">@glossary

**Heat** — The running tally of attention you've drawn.

**Hard Choice** — A roll of 6–10. The fiction advances, but it costs you.

@end-glossary
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-terms" data-source-range="289:290"><p><strong>Heat</strong> — The running tally of attention you’ve drawn.</p>
<p><strong>Hard Choice</strong> — A roll of 6–10. The fiction advances, but it costs you.</p>
</div></div>
<h2>Procedure</h2>
<p>A zero-padded step list for anything the table runs in order.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="304:314" class="language-markdown">@procedure

1. **The Dream Master describes the situation.** Where you are, what's wrong.
2. **A Dreamer declares an action.** Say what your character does.
3. **If the outcome is uncertain, ROLL THE DIE!**
4. **The result determines what happens next.**

@end-procedure
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<ol class="dc-steps">
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">01</span><span><strong>The Dream Master describes the situation.</strong> Where you are, what’s wrong.</span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">02</span><span><strong>A Dreamer declares an action.</strong> Say what your character does.</span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">03</span><span><strong>If the outcome is uncertain, <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span></strong></span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">04</span><span><strong>The result determines what happens next.</strong></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<h2>Outcome ladder</h2>
<p>The d20 result table every roll resolves against. One row per line: <code>roll | name | text</code>. Rows are colored by position, top to bottom: crit, hit, mixed, miss, fail. <code>@outcome flush</code> runs it edge to edge.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="334:345" class="language-markdown">@outcome

20 | Triumph | Best-case outcome. You do it, and the moment breaks your way.
11–19 | Success | You do it. Clean.
6–10 | Hard Choice | You succeed, but it costs you.
2–5 | Failure | You don't get what you wanted.
1 | Catastrophe | It goes bad, and then worse.

@end-outcome
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-outcomes" data-source-range="350:351">  <div class="dc-outcomes-label">Outcomes</div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row crit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-crit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Triumph</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">20</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">Best-case outcome. You do it, and the moment breaks your way.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row hit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-hit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Success</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">11–19</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You do it. Clean.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row mixed">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-mixed"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hard Choice</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">6–10</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You succeed, but it costs you.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row miss">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-miss"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Failure</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">2–5</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You don’t get what you wanted.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row fail">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-fail"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Catastrophe</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">1</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It goes bad, and then worse.</span>
  </div>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-outcomes-surface</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-border</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-label-bg</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-label-color</code>, <code>--dc-outcome-key-color</code>, <code>--dc-outcome-name-color</code>.</p>
<h3>A table of the five bands is the ladder</h3>
<p>Write the result table you would write anyway. A two-column pipe table whose first column holds exactly the five standard bands (<code>20</code>, <code>11-19</code>, <code>6-10</code>, <code>2-5</code>, <code>1</code>, in either order) is drawn as the same ladder, in a skill card or anywhere else. The headers do not matter (<code>Roll | Result</code>, <code>Result | Effect</code>, or empty), and a band is read however it is typed: <code>11-19</code>, <code>11 – 19</code>, <code>**20**</code>. Any other set of bands (six bands, a split at 10) is not a ladder, and stays a plain table: for those, use <code>@roll-table</code> below.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="368:377" class="language-markdown">| Roll | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 20 | The jammed door gives. Something comes with it. |
| 11–19 | The door opens. |
| 6–10 | It opens, loudly. |
| 2–5 | It holds. |
| 1 | The handle comes off in your hand. |
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-outcomes">
  <div class="dc-outcomes-label">Outcomes</div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row crit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-crit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Crit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">20</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The jammed door gives. Something comes with it.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row hit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-hit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">11–19</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The door opens.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row mixed">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-mixed"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hard Choice</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">6–10</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It opens, loudly.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row miss">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-miss"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Miss</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">2–5</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It holds.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row fail">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-fail"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Catastrophe</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">1</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The handle comes off in your hand.</span>
  </div>
</div>
</div>
<h2>Roll table</h2>
<p>A random table that is not the ladder: one die, equal-weight rows, no tier colours. Wrap a pipe table in <code>@roll-table</code>; <code>label</code> names it and <code>die</code> picks the die on the chip (<code>d20</code> when you write none). Put the range in the first column (<code>1-4</code>, <code>5-8</code>, …) and the result in the second.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="396:409" class="language-markdown">@roll-table label=&quot;Pocket Lint&quot; die=d20

| Roll | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 1-4 | A bent key that opens nothing nearby. |
| 5-8 | Half a noodle voucher, still valid. |
| 9-12 | A dead earbud with a name scratched in. |
| 13-16 | A folded map of a different district. |
| 17-20 | A battered signal flare. |

@end-roll-table
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-roll-table" data-label="Pocket Lint" data-die="d20" data-source-range="414:415"><div class="dc-roll-table-caption"><span class="dc-roll-table-die">d20</span><span class="dc-roll-table-name">Pocket Lint</span></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="416:417">
<tr data-source-range="416:417">
<th>Roll</th>
<th>Result</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="418:423">
<tr data-source-range="418:419">
<td>1-4</td>
<td>A bent key that opens nothing nearby.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="419:420">
<td>5-8</td>
<td>Half a noodle voucher, still valid.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="420:421">
<td>9-12</td>
<td>A dead earbud with a name scratched in.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="421:422">
<td>13-16</td>
<td>A folded map of a different district.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="422:423">
<td>17-20</td>
<td>A battered signal flare.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-roll-table-accent</code> (frame, caption and range rule), <code>--dc-roll-table-surface</code>, <code>--dc-roll-table-head-bg</code>.</p>
<h2>Card</h2>
<p>A choice card: heading, body, and a closing quote that becomes the card’s footer. Wrap a run of cards in <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code>, or <code>@dreams</code> to color the accent for that step of character creation.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="436:451" class="language-markdown">@flaws

@card

### Addictive Personality

You chase instant gratification without weighing the consequences. The city always has one more hit on offer.

&gt; &quot;Just one more, and then I'll quit.&quot;

@end-card

@end-flaws
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-flaws" data-source-range="456:457"><div class="dc-card" data-source-range="458:459"><div class="dc-card-body">
<h3>Addictive Personality</h3>
<p>You chase instant gratification without weighing the consequences. The city always has one more hit on offer.</p>
<blockquote class="dc-card-footer">
<p data-source-range="464:465">“Just one more, and then I’ll quit.”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div></div></div>
<p>The same card under <code>.dc-ideals</code> takes the ideal accent:</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-ideals" data-source-range="476:477"><div class="dc-card" data-source-range="478:479"><div class="dc-card-body">
<h3>Honor</h3>
<p>You believe in a code, and upholding it is your duty no matter the cost.</p>
<blockquote class="dc-card-footer">
<p data-source-range="484:485">“I made a promise to help those in need, and I’ll keep it at all costs.”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div></div></div>
<h2>Gear</h2>
<p>An item entry: a display-face name, an italic tagline of tags, then the mechanics. Separate entries with <code>---</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="496:519">@gear

### Throwaway Blaster

*Ranged. Pistol. CQC Locked, Pack Fed.*

**Range** Reach to Near only. **Damage** 2 on a Hit, 4 on a Triumph. Disposable after the run; serial-clean and untraceable.

@end-gear

---

@gear

### Snake Cable

*Utility. Mechanized. Reach 50 ft.*

A 50-foot length of mechanized links that coils itself, climbs, anchors, or cinches around an object.

@end-gear
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-card dc-gear" data-source-range="524:525"><h3>Throwaway Blaster</h3>
<p><em>Ranged. Pistol. CQC Locked, Pack Fed.</em></p>
<p><strong>Range</strong> Reach to Near only. <strong>Damage</strong> 2 on a Hit, 4 on a Triumph. Disposable after the run; serial-clean and untraceable.</p>
</div><hr data-source-range="534:535">
<div class="dc-card dc-gear" data-source-range="536:537"><h3>Snake Cable</h3>
<p><em>Utility. Mechanized. Reach 50 ft.</em></p>
<p>A 50-foot length of mechanized links that coils itself, climbs, anchors, or cinches around an object.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Gear is a card with an orange rail. The name, the italic tagline and the bold price line are picked out by their shape, and a <code>@callout gear label=&quot;SPECS&quot;</code> at the foot sits a gap below the body. A long run packs tighter two-up: put the entries in <code>@columns 2</code> inside a <code>@section</code>. Entries fill the first column, then the second, and a section splits by default, so a run longer than a page breaks between entries. <a href="#ch-example-gear-tech" data-gp-source-token="[Gear &amp; Tech](#ch-example-gear-tech)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Gear &amp; Tech</a> shows gear in columns.</p>
<h2>Tables</h2>
<p>Pipe tables carry every lookup: augment slots, weapon lists, roll tables, option tables, NPC statlines. No class, no macro.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="556:563" class="language-markdown">| Augment | Slot | Cost | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| Reflex Booster | Legs | 1,200 | +2 to initiative rolls |
| Subdermal Plating | Torso | 1,800 | Reduce bludgeoning damage by 1 |
| Optic Splice | Head | 900 | Ignore darkness penalties |
</code></pre>
